  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nAs of 1am, a storm cluster consisting of moderate rain and a few \nlightning strikes are tracking eastward along/ahead of the frontal \nboundary over north central SD and into ND where forcing is better, \nhowever, CAPE and lapse rates are pretty weak with decent bulk shear \nbetween 30-40kts. None of the CAMs really have a good handle, either \nnot showing this storm at all or should be dissipating by now. So \nif it holds together it will continue to skirt along the ND/SD \nborder bringing the potential for lighting and brief heavy \ndownpours. Areas of light rain/drizzle per WAA flow continues over \nfar northeastern SD into western MN as well. 00Z ABR sounding \nindicated a nearly saturated sounding, especially at the lower \nlevels, per southeasterly flow near the surface and southwesterly \nflow between 700-500mb. This is causing fog/low stratus over \nportions of northeastern SD/western MN per latest obs and webcams \nshowing a reduction in visibility and MVFR/IFR cigs. So have fog \nmention in the grids through the mid morning.\n\nWithin this southwest flow, weak embedded shortwave energy \ncontinues to track across the Northern Plains with a more notable \nshortwave to the south of the CWA in Nebraska tracking \neast/northeast. An inverted trough/weak low is currently over \ncentral ND with its frontal boundary extending into central SD and \nits weak low lying along the boundary. By 12Z, the low is forecast \nto over northeastern ND/MN with the cold front extending through \nnortheastern SD into NE, with only a slight movement northeast of \nthe low and east with the cold front. Models indicate scattered \nchances of showers and thunderstorms here and there ahead and along \nthis boundary, mainly over south central through northeastern SD/MN \nwhere better forcing resides, however, differences in timing and \nlocation do vary between the CAMs so stuck mostly to the NBM which \nkeeps it more broadbrushed. CAPE values this afternoon will be \naround 1000 j/kg or less and shear under 30kts, therefore, any \nstorms that occur will be short lived and below severe limits as \nlapse rates remain weak. However, monsoonal moisture being pulled in \nper southwest flow is causing PWAT values of 1.7 to almost 2\", west \nto east across the CWA, so any rain shower and/or weak thunderstorms \n(especially slow moving) could produce heavy rainfall. \n\nBy this evening, the region will be behind the cold front, in \nnorthwest flow at the surface and winds switching to zonal aloft. A \nhigh pressure system will track into the region from the northwest \nand center itself over the Dakotas Sunday keeping the area dry. This \nhigh will shift a bit east Sunday night with pops returning over \ncentral SD (40-65%) Sunday night into Monday morning. As the high \nexits the region, a stronger shortwave and its west to east oriented \ncold front will drop out of Canada and over the region bringing our \nbest chance for moisture with widespread pops of 50-70%. Still a few \ndays out to nail down specifics or whether severe weather will be a \nthreat.\n\nTemperatures remain around average to slightly below average through \nthe weekend, ranging in the 70s to around 80. Little bit warmer on \nMonday with highs in the upper 70s to the upper 80s before cooling \nback into the 70s behind the cold front and incoming high Tuesday \nand Wednesday.",
